  "prompt": "If I manage to survive for an hour playing hide and seek at my alma mater high school with a towering murderer who is 190cm tall and weighs 90kg, I’d win $5,000,000. However, if I get caught, I would be brutally killed. Even if the seeker catches me, it’s still considered a success if I can escape and last for the hour. From a probability standpoint, is it more advantageous to take the bet or not?",
  "answer": "The key unknown is the chance you survive the full hour, including escaping if caught. If the $5 million is paid only on survival and there’s no other cost, the bet’s expected payout is **$5,000,000 × your survival probability**. But that calculation leaves out the cost of being killed: there’s no reasonable way to treat that outcome as an ordinary financial loss or to assign it a reliable dollar value.\n\nA 190 cm, 90 kg seeker’s size alone doesn’t tell us your odds. The school layout, your familiarity with it, the seeker’s capabilities, and the rules matter far more. Still, with a real chance of being hunted and brutally killed, this is not a sensible bet to take for money. If this is a real offer or threat, don’t participate; get to safety and contact emergency services.",
